<p><img decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/2026-10-24-to-be-precise-1.jpg" alt="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg" width="960" height="766" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-74483" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/2026-10-24-to-be-precise-1.jpg 960w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/2026-10-24-to-be-precise-1-600x479.jpg 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/2026-10-24-to-be-precise-1-300x239.jpg 300w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 960px" /></p>
<p>On what date did Vincent van Gogh paint his 1889 masterpiece <em>The Starry Night</em>? Earlier studies had placed the creation in mid-June of that year, based on a letter the artist had written to his brother Theo around June 18. But Kyung Hee University doctoral candidate Elijah Jeong-hyun Kim noted that an observer in that period should have seen a waning gibbous moon, not the crescent seen in the painting.</p>
<p>Intrigued, he began to compare astronomical simulations, weather records, and van Gogh&#8217;s other writings. A better estimate of the scene&#8217;s creation, he says, is dawn on July 22, 1889. On that date the crescent moon, Venus, and the Hyades star cluster in the constellation Taurus would have been visible from van Gogh&#8217;s window in southern France, and the recorded hot weather would have inspired the cicadas the artist mentions in a letter.</p>
<p>Kim&#8217;s article appears in the journal <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-73025-5"><em>Scientific Reports</em></a>. &#8220;Our conversation with Van Gogh&#8217;s other skies is still ongoing,&#8221; <a href="https://www.popsci.com/science/starry-night-van-gogh-date/">he told <em>Popular Science</em></a>. &#8220;We plan to tell those stories one by one.&#8221;</p>
<p>(Texas State University &#8220;forensic astronomer&#8221; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Olson_(astronomer)">Donald W. Olson</a> has done similar work, <a href="https://www.space.com/27007-astronomy-sleuths-monet-painting.html">estimating for example</a> that the scene below depicts Le Havre harbor as seen from Claude Monet&#8217;s hotel room at 7:35 a.m. on Nov. 13, 1872.) (Thanks, Sharon.)</p>
